course: dictionary
lesson: 87
title: Actually: what is really true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Actually means «in fact» or «really». It tells you what is true. «It's actually quite easy.» The word shows that the answer may be different from what people expect. Try reading the sentence without actually and notice what changes.
explain: Actually also marks a surprise or a correction. «The Sun looks small but is actually huge.» Your eyes say small, but the truth is huge. «Some design stories grow bigger than the truth, so it is worth checking what the designer actually said.» Here actually points to what was really said, not what people think they heard.
explain: Actually can also sound soft and polite. When you disagree, you can say, «I think it's a dog, but actually it might be a fox.» This is gentle, because you are not saying the other person is wrong. Phrases like «I think…» and «Did you actually…?» often use it too.
fact: Actually means «in fact» or «really».
fact: Actually shows that something is true even when people expect something else.
fact: Actually can correct someone or show a surprise, as in «The Sun looks small but is actually huge.»
fact: Actually can sound polite when you disagree or change your mind.
fact: Check it by swapping in «really» or «in fact»: if the sentence still makes sense, actually has the right meaning.
exercise: «It's actually quite easy.» What does actually mean here? | It is easy to find | Maybe it is hard | It is easy to lift | *In fact, it is really easy
exercise: «The Sun looks small but is actually huge.» What does actually show? | The Sun is cold | A question about the time | The Sun is getting smaller | *The truth, which is surprising
exercise: Ella says, «I think you're right, but actually I see it differently.» Why does she use actually? | To shout loudly | *To disagree gently and politely | To ask about the time | To say goodbye
test: «A school legend is usually told about a kid at my cousin's school, never about someone you can actually ask.» What does actually mean here? | A person who is very tall | A person who has left school | *Really, a person you can truly ask | A person who is asleep
test: Which sentence uses actually to show a surprise? | "I like football." | "The shop is closed on Sundays." | "Please pass the bread." | *"The water looks cold, but it is actually warm."
exam: «Good listening means answering about what they actually said.» Which word could replace actually here? | *Really | Loudly | Never | Later
